      "type": "doc",
      "title": "Learning Doc",
      "content": {
        "doc_md": "Comments are used to explain the CSS code, and may help when you edit the \nsource code at a later date.\n\nComments are also used to temporarily disable sections of CSS code within a \nstylesheet.\n\nComments are ignored by browsers!\n\nA CSS comment is placed inside the HTML<style>element, and starts with/*and ends with*/:\n\nYou can add comments wherever you want in the code:\n\nEven in the middle of a code line:\n\nComments can also span \nmultiple lines:\n\nFrom the HTML tutorial, you learned that you can add comments to your HTML source by using the<!--...-->syntax.\n\nIn the following example, we use a combination of HTML and CSS comments:\n\n### Code Snippets\n\n```\n/* This is a single-line comment */\np\n{\ncolor: red;\n}\n\n---\n\np\n{\ncolor: red;\u00a0\n  /* Set text color to red */\n}\n\n---\n\np\n{\ncolor: /*red*/blue;\n}\n\n---\n\n/* This is\na multi-line\ncomment */\np\n{\ncolor: red;\n}\n\n---\n\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<style>\np {\ncolor: red; /* Set \n  text color to red */\n}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<h2>My \n  Heading</h2>\n<!-- These paragraphs will be red -->\n<p>Hello \n  World!</p>\n<p>This paragraph is styled with CSS.</p>\n<p>HTML \n  and CSS comments are \n  not shown in the output.</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n```"
      }
    },
